      'Netflix vs. Blockbuster: Category Creation Through Positioning',
      [
        { date: '1997-2002', event: 'Netflix launches DVD-by-mail, positioning against late fees and inconvenience', context: 'Reed Hastings didn\'t try to out-Blockbuster Blockbuster. He identified the two biggest customer pain points, late fees ($800M/year for Blockbuster) and driving to stores, and built a model that eliminated both. Netflix positioned itself as the anti-Blockbuster: no fees, no trips, unlimited selection.' },
        { date: '2003-2006', event: 'Blockbuster launches "Total Access", combining online and in-store returns', context: 'Blockbuster\'s positioning dilemma: its 9,000 stores were simultaneously its biggest strength (brand, convenience) and biggest weakness (massive fixed costs). The hybrid "Total Access" program was actually beating Netflix in subscriber growth by 2007, but the $400M annual store costs made it financially unsustainable.' },
        { date: '2007-2010', event: 'Netflix pivots to streaming, creating an entirely new category', context: 'Instead of competing in the DVD market (Red Ocean), Netflix created a Blue Ocean: unlimited streaming for $7.99/month. Blockbuster couldn\'t follow, its stores, inventory, and business model were built for physical media. Netflix didn\'t beat Blockbuster at its own game; it changed the game entirely.' },
        { date: '2010-2024', event: 'Blockbuster files bankruptcy; Netflix reaches $34B revenue and 260M+ subscribers', context: 'Netflix\'s competitive strategy followed a classic pattern: first, identify the incumbent\'s weakness that\'s also tied to their strength (stores = brand AND cost burden). Then, position in a way that turns that strength into a disadvantage. Blockbuster\'s stores went from asset to anchor.' }
      ],
      'Netflix\'s genius wasn\'t building a better video rental company, it was refusing to be a video rental company at all. Each strategic pivot (mail → streaming → original content) repositioned Netflix in a Blue Ocean where Blockbuster\'s strengths became irrelevant. The lesson: the best competitive strategy often isn\'t beating rivals, it\'s making them compete in a game they can\'t win.'
