      ['What is product-led growth (PLG)?', 'A GTM strategy where the product itself drives acquisition, conversion, and expansion. Users discover, adopt, and invite others without traditional sales. Examples: Slack, Zoom, Figma, Notion, Canva.', { stat: 'Slack: 8M users, 0 reps', sprite: 'magnet' }],
      ['What are the four main pricing strategies?', 'Penetration (low price to capture share), Skimming (high initial price for early adopters), Freemium (free basic + paid premium), and Value-based (price based on customer value delivered).', { stat: '4 ways to name one number', sprite: 'price-tag' }],
      ['Why did Google+ fail despite Google\'s resources?', 'No product-market fit. Google forced adoption through integration with Gmail and YouTube, but users didn\'t want another social network, Facebook already had their friends. No GTM strategy fixes a product nobody wants.', { stat: '$585M spent, near zero used', sprite: 'question' }],
      ['What is Net Revenue Retention (NRR)?', 'Measures if existing customers spend more or less over time. Above 100% means revenue grows without new customers. Snowflake\'s 158% NRR means customers spend 58% more annually, compounding growth engine.', { stat: '158% means +58% from the same customers', sprite: 'chart-up' }],
      ['What is the difference between direct and indirect distribution channels?', 'Direct: your website, app, sales team (higher margins, more control). Indirect: retailers, distributors, affiliates, marketplaces (broader reach, lower margins). Best GTM strategies combine both.', { stat: 'Retail keeps 40 to 60 cents', sprite: 'arrow-split' }]
