      ['When should you blitzscale?', 'Only in winner-take-all markets with strong network effects and sufficient capital. If the market doesn\'t have network effects (customers switch easily), blitzscaling just burns cash without building a defensible position.', { stat: 'Uber burned $20B+ to hold 72% share', sprite: 'stopwatch' }],
      ['What are the four types of network effects?', 'Direct (more users = better for each user), Indirect/two-sided (more of side A benefits side B), Data (more usage = better product), and Platform (more developers = more users = more developers).', { stat: '4 types: direct, two-sided, data, platform', sprite: 'network' }],
      ['What is critical mass?', 'The minimum scale where network effects become self-sustaining. Facebook needed 70%+ adoption at each college. Uber needed enough drivers for 3-minute pickup times. Below critical mass, growth requires subsidies; above it, growth is organic.', { stat: '70% of a college, or a 3-minute pickup', sprite: 'scale-tip' }],
      ['What killed Pets.com despite $300M in revenue?', 'Negative unit economics: they lost 27 cents on every dollar sold. Scaling multiplied losses. Revenue without positive margins is just subsidized destruction. Unit economics must work before scaling.', { stat: '$1.00 in, $1.27 out, on every single sale', sprite: 'scales' }]
