            { label: 'Maintain pricing but accelerate product differentiation, build features their free product can\'t easily replicate (advanced analytics, AI automation, enterprise security, integrations), and leverage your existing user community as a moat', consequence: 'Strategic excellence. Free competitors typically offer basic functionality without depth. By deepening your product in areas that matter most to paying customers (analytics, security, automation), you create differentiation that justifies the price premium. Your community of 50,000 users is an asset, templates, workflows, and institutional knowledge that a new free tool can\'t replicate. This is how Slack survived Microsoft Teams launching for free.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Immediately raise a Series B to outspend the competitor on marketing', consequence: 'Raising emergency capital to fund a marketing war against a better-funded competitor is desperation, not strategy. VCs will see the competitive threat and offer worse terms. The answer isn\'t more money, it\'s better positioning and deeper product value.', score: 1 }
