          options: [
            { label: 'Take the deal, $5M/year is too significant to pass up', consequence: 'The $5M is tempting, but pulling engineers off the core product for 6 months stalls your scaling momentum at the worst possible time. Network effects are compounding, every month of stalled product development costs you exponentially. Plus, custom enterprise features often don\'t benefit other customers, reducing overall product velocity.', score: 1 },
            { label: 'Counter with a deal that works for both: build the features they need as platform capabilities that benefit all enterprise users, charge $3M/year non-exclusively, and maintain your product roadmap with a 3-month timeline for the critical features', consequence: 'Masterful negotiation. By building their requirements as platform features (not custom code), every enterprise customer benefits. Non-exclusive means you can sell the same capabilities to their competitors. A shorter timeline keeps momentum. And $3M/year for a scalable enterprise feature set creates a template for future Fortune 100 deals, a repeatable revenue stream worth $50M+ annually.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Reject entirely, enterprise deals are a distraction from product-led growth', consequence: 'At $15M ARR with network effects compounding, the right enterprise deals can add $30-50M in predictable revenue. Enterprise contracts have 95%+ retention rates and dramatically improve unit economics. The key is structuring deals that enhance the platform rather than creating custom dead-ends.', score: 0 }
          ]
