      [
        { date: '2006', event: 'Disney acquires Pixar for $7.4 billion', context: 'Disney Animation was struggling, its last hit was 1994\'s Lion King. Pixar had produced 7 consecutive #1 films. CEO Bob Iger recognized that buying creative talent was more valuable than trying to rebuild it internally. John Lasseter became Chief Creative Officer and revitalized Disney Animation, producing Frozen, Tangled, and Moana.' },
        { date: '2009', event: 'Disney acquires Marvel Entertainment for $4 billion', context: 'Wall Street was skeptical, Marvel\'s characters were considered niche "comic book stuff." But Iger saw that Marvel owned 5,000+ characters and a storytelling system (interconnected cinematic universe) that no competitor could replicate. The MCU has since grossed $30B+ at the box office, with Avengers: Endgame alone earning $2.8B.' },
        { date: '2012', event: 'Disney acquires Lucasfilm for $4.05 billion', context: 'George Lucas personally chose Disney to continue the Star Wars legacy. Disney got Star Wars, Indiana Jones, Industrial Light & Magic, and Skywalker Sound. The Force Awakens alone grossed $2.07B, recouping half the purchase price in one film. Star Wars theme park expansions generate billions in recurring revenue.' },
        { date: '2019', event: 'Disney acquires 21st Century Fox entertainment assets for $71.3 billion', context: 'The largest deal, giving Disney the X-Men, Avatar, The Simpsons, National Geographic, and a controlling stake in Hulu. This acquisition was strategic for Disney+ by providing the content volume needed to compete with Netflix. Disney+ reached 160M+ subscribers within 3 years of launch, fueled by content from all four acquisitions.' }
      ],
      'Disney\'s M&A strategy succeeded where others failed because Bob Iger followed three principles: (1) buy irreplaceable IP that can be monetized across multiple platforms (films, merchandise, theme parks, streaming), (2) preserve the creative culture of acquired companies rather than absorbing them into Disney\'s bureaucracy, and (3) pay fair prices for assets with long-duration value, IP that will generate revenue for decades, not years. The $86B invested has returned multiples in box office, merchandise, theme parks, and streaming subscriber growth.'
