      'Google\'s Project Aristotle studied 180 teams and found that psychological safety, not individual talent, was the #1 predictor of team performance.',
      'By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how organizational structure shapes strategy execution, why culture is a company\'s most powerful competitive advantage, and how the best leaders build teams that consistently outperform, even with less talent.',
      'Organizational structure comparison showing functional, divisional, matrix, and flat structures with performance metrics for each',
      { type: 'comparison', title: 'Project Aristotle: Top Predictors of Team Success', rows: [{ label: '1. Psychological safety', value: 'Members feel safe to take risks', explain: 'Google went looking for the smartest people and found something else sitting on top: whether you can say I do not understand out loud. That is a property of the room, not of any single person in it.' }, { label: '2. Dependability', value: 'Members deliver quality work on time', explain: 'Second place is not skill either. It is whether the work lands when you said it would. Both of the top two are about trust between people, not about what any one person knows.' }, { label: '3. Structure and clarity', value: 'Clear roles, plans, and goals', explain: 'Third is simply knowing who decides what. All three are things a manager sets up before the work starts, and the footer is the punchline: individual talent did not make the top five at all.' }], footer: 'Individual talent was NOT in the top 5 predictors of team performance', sentiment: 'positive' }
