      { label: 'Toxic culture, rigid structure', detail: 'WeWork under Adam Neumann had a culture of excess, zero accountability, and top-down decision-making. The company burned through $14B in funding, had no path to profitability, and its IPO imploded when investors saw the dysfunction. Peak valuation of $47B crashed to $9B at IPO, then to near-zero. Culture killed a company that had genuine market potential.' },
      { label: 'Intentional culture, adaptive structure', detail: 'Netflix\'s culture deck, called "the most important document to come out of Silicon Valley" by Sheryl Sandberg, built a system of radical transparency, high performance, and freedom with responsibility. The "keeper test" (would I fight to keep this person?) ensures every team member is exceptional. Result: $34B revenue, 260M+ subscribers, consistent innovation.' },
      { withoutVisual: { type: 'flow', title: 'WeWork: Culture of Excess', nodes: [{ label: '$47B peak valuation', sprite: 'rocket', explain: 'That was more than Boeing was worth at the time, for a company renting out desks in buildings it did not own. The number came from the story, not from the accounts.' }, { label: 'Zero accountability culture', sprite: 'chain-break', explain: 'Adam Neumann leased buildings he personally owned back to the company, and the company paid him $5.9M for the trademark on the word We. Nobody on the org chart could stop either one.' }, { label: 'IPO implosion, near-zero', sprite: 'chart-down', explain: 'The IPO filing was the first time outsiders got to read those governance terms. Six weeks later the offering was pulled and Neumann was out as CEO.' }, { label: '$47B → ~$0', sprite: 'bear', explain: 'The desks, the leases and the members were identical on both ends of that arrow. What changed was whether anyone was allowed to tell the CEO no.' }], sentiment: 'negative', sprite: 'crack' }, withVisual: { type: 'flow', title: 'Netflix: Culture as Strategy', nodes: [{ label: 'Radical transparency', sprite: 'chat', explain: 'Employees see the subscriber numbers before the earnings call, and feedback is expected to run upward as well as down. The bet is that adults with the full picture decide better than a manager relaying half of it.' }, { label: 'Keeper test for talent', sprite: 'scales', explain: 'One question, asked by every manager about every report: would I fight to keep this person. A no costs the company four to six months of severance, which is the price of not letting mediocrity settle in.' }, { label: '260M+ subscribers, $34B rev', sprite: 'stack-cash', explain: 'Roughly 13,000 employees produce that revenue, about $1.3M each. Traditional media runs closer to $250K per person with far more staff and far more buildings.' }, { label: 'Culture = competitive moat', sprite: 'lock', explain: 'A rival can copy the pay, the culture deck and the keeper test in one afternoon. What cannot be copied is ten years of only exceptional people choosing to stay.' }], sentiment: 'positive', sprite: 'shield' } }
