        { term: 'A/B Test', definition: 'A controlled experiment comparing two versions (A and B) of a variable to determine which performs better on a defined metric.', example: 'Google tested 41 shades of blue for ad links. The winning shade increased clicks enough to generate $200M in additional annual revenue.' },
        { term: 'Statistical Significance', definition: 'The probability that an observed result is not due to random chance. Standard threshold: 95% confidence (p < 0.05).', example: 'An A/B test showing 5% conversion improvement with p=0.03 means there\'s only a 3% chance the result is random, statistically significant at the 95% threshold.' },
        { term: 'Survivorship Bias', definition: 'Focusing on entities that survived a selection process while ignoring those that didn\'t, leading to false conclusions about success factors.', example: 'Studying only successful startups and concluding "risk-taking leads to success" ignores the thousands of risk-taking startups that failed. The survivors are not representative.' },
        { term: 'Correlation vs. Causation', definition: 'Two variables moving together (correlation) does not mean one causes the other. A third variable or coincidence may explain the relationship.', example: 'Countries with more Nobel Prize winners also consume more chocolate. Chocolate doesn\'t cause genius, wealth correlates with both education spending and chocolate consumption.' }
