        'Metrics fall into two categories: lagging indicators (measure outcomes that already happened, revenue, profit, churn rate) and leading indicators (predict future outcomes, sign-ups, engagement, NPS). The best data-driven companies focus on leading indicators because they allow proactive decisions. By the time a lagging indicator (revenue decline) shows up, it\'s often too late. Leading indicators (declining engagement, rising complaint volume) provide early warning.',
        'The key business metrics by function: Growth (CAC, conversion rate, viral coefficient), Retention (churn rate, NPS, DAU/MAU ratio), Revenue (ARPU, LTV, MRR/ARR), Product (feature adoption, time-to-value, support tickets), and Operations (efficiency ratio, cycle time, defect rate). The most dangerous metric is vanity metrics, numbers that look impressive but don\'t drive decisions. Total registered users is vanity; daily active users is actionable. Total revenue is vanity; unit economics is actionable.',
        'Data literacy means understanding that all data tells a story, but not all stories are true. Simpson\'s Paradox shows that a trend appearing in disaggregated data can reverse when the data is combined. A famous example: UC Berkeley appeared to discriminate against women in admissions (overall acceptance rate lower for women). But department by department, women had HIGHER acceptance rates. The paradox: women applied disproportionately to more competitive departments. The aggregated data told the opposite story from the disaggregated truth. Always segment data before drawing conclusions.'
